Memory is persistent state with a friendly name. Treat it like a database feature, not a larger prompt. Every remembered fact should have a scope, source, freshness rule, and correction path.

## Separate memory types

Working context belongs to one run. Session memory supports a bounded conversation. Long-term memory survives sessions and needs stronger consent, access, and deletion rules. Operational state—such as an approval or workflow status—should live in a typed system of record, not only in retrieved text.

```ts
type Memory = {
  value: string;
  scope: "run" | "session" | "user";
  source: string;
  observedAt: string;
  expiresAt?: string;
  confidence: "verified" | "inferred";
};
```

Log which memories were retrieved and which affected a decision. This makes [AI agent architecture](/blog/ai-agent-architecture) inspectable and complements [prompt versioning](/blog/prompt-versioning-and-prompt-management) when context changes behavior.

## Design for correction

Users change their minds and systems change their data. Allow a memory to be corrected, expired, or deleted. Prefer source references over copied sensitive content. If the agent cannot explain where a fact came from, it should ask or abstain instead of presenting memory as truth.

## Measure memory, not just recall

Track retrieval rate, useful-memory rate, stale-memory incidents, correction rate, context growth, and task outcome. More recalled facts are not automatically better. The target is the smallest relevant context that improves a user-visible result without creating surprising behavior.
